steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1789901195
      nanos: 765055473
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "4f917d54-c553-47b5-92e6-7ba603c32a73"
      step_index: 197
      metadata_index: 184
      cascade_id: "4f917d54-c553-47b5-92e6-7ba603c32a73"
    }
    internal_metadata {
      status_transitions {
        updated_status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
        timestamp {
          seconds: 1789901195
          nanos: 765069333
        }
      }
    }
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "FINAL SENIOR ENGINEERING + DESIGN DIRECTIVE\n\nTreat the current Pearls Elegance Thrift Abaya Wears website as a production project being reviewed by a team of top-tier engineers, product designers, UX specialists, performance engineers, and creative directors.\n\nDo NOT make superficial cosmetic changes.\n\nI want the final website to give the impression:\n\n\342\200\234An exceptional engineering and design team worked obsessively on every detail.\342\200\235\n\nThe quality should be visible in both the DESIGN and the ENGINEERING.\n\n==================================================\n1. THINK LIKE A PRINCIPAL ENGINEER\n==================================================\n\nBefore changing anything, inspect the existing codebase carefully.\n\nUnderstand:\n- application structure\n- components\n- state management\n- product data\n- routing\n- responsive behavior\n- WhatsApp integration\n- inquiry basket\n- SEO implementation\n- image handling\n- dependencies\n- loading behavior\n- error states\n\nDo not blindly rewrite working code.\n\nKeep strong existing architecture.\n\nRefactor only where doing so creates a real improvement.\n\nEliminate:\n- duplicated logic\n- unnecessary components\n- dead code\n- inconsistent patterns\n- fragile state\n- unnecessary dependencies\n- console errors\n- warnings\n- broken imports\n- hardcoded behavior that should be reusable\n\nCreate clean, predictable, maintainable code.\n\n==================================================\n2. THINK LIKE A STAFF / PRINCIPAL FRONTEND ENGINEER\n==================================================\n\nEvery interaction should feel deliberate.\n\nAudit:\n- navigation\n- buttons\n- product cards\n- filters\n- search\n- inquiry drawer\n- WhatsApp flow\n- modals\n- forms\n- sticky elements\n- mobile navigation\n- page transitions\n- hover states\n- loading states\n- empty states\n- error states\n\nNothing should feel accidental.\n\nEvery interaction should have:\n- clear feedback\n- appropriate timing\n- correct state handling\n- accessibility\n- mobile support\n\nDo not add animation just for the sake of animation.\n\n==================================================\n3. THINK LIKE A WORLD-CLASS PRODUCT DESIGNER\n==================================================\n\nThe layout must have intentional hierarchy.\n\nAsk of every section:\n\nWhy is this here?\n\nWhat should the user notice first?\n\nWhat action should the user take?\n\nDoes this section deserve this much visual space?\n\nDoes it contribute to the shopping experience?\n\nRemove anything that does not serve a purpose.\n\nUse:\n- strong visual hierarchy\n- exceptional spacing\n- consistent alignment\n- disciplined typography\n- controlled contrast\n- visual rhythm\n- intentional whitespace\n\nThe site should feel designed, not assembled.\n\n==================================================\n4. PREMIUM FASHION ART DIRECTION\n==================================================\n\nThe visual language must remain:\n\nPremium\nEditorial\nMinimal\nElegant\nModern\nWarm\nSophisticated\n\nClothing MUST remain on mannequins / dress forms.\n\nNO human models.\n\nProduct photography should feel like a professional fashion studio.\n\nUse consistent:\n- lighting\n- composition\n- image ratios\n- background treatment\n- scale\n- cropping\n\nDo not use random images merely to fill empty areas.\n\nEvery image should have a reason to exist.\n\n==================================================\n5. MAKE THE HOMEPAGE FEEL EXPENSIVE\n==================================================\n\nThe homepage should not feel like:\n\nHero\n\342\206\222 cards\n\342\206\222 cards\n\342\206\222 cards\n\342\206\222 footer\n\nInstead, create visual storytelling.\n\nRecommended rhythm:\n\nEDITORIAL HERO\n\n\342\206\223\nNEW ARRIVALS\n\n\342\206\223\nCURATED COLLECTIONS\n\n\342\206\223\nEDITORIAL BRAND STATEMENT\n\n\342\206\223\nSELECTED PRODUCTS\n\n\342\206\223\nCUSTOMER TESTIMONIALS\n\n\342\206\223\nWHOLESALE / RESELLER\n\n\342\206\223\nSTORE LOCATION\n\n\342\206\223\nFINAL CALL TO ACTION\n\nEach section should have a distinct visual purpose.\n\nAvoid repeating the same card design endlessly.\n\n==================================================\n6. PRODUCT EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nThis is still an e-commerce/catalog website.\n\nProduct discovery must be excellent.\n\nMake it extremely easy to:\n\nDiscover\n\342\206\222 Browse\n\342\206\222 Filter\n\342\206\222 Open product\n\342\206\222 Check price\n\342\206\222 Add to inquiry\n\342\206\222 Contact seller\n\nProduct pages should feel intentional and premium.\n\nUse large photography.\n\nDo not overwhelm shoppers with UI.\n\nThe product is the hero.\n\n==================================================\n7. WHATSAPP EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nThe WhatsApp integration should feel like a first-class commerce feature.\n\nDo not make it look like an external link slapped onto the site.\n\nThe flow should feel seamless:\n\nORDER ON WHATSAPP\n\n\342\206\223\n\nPre-filled message\n\n\342\206\223\n\nCustomer confirms availability with Pearls Elegance\n\nThe inquiry basket should feel like a sophisticated lightweight cart.\n\nIt should support:\n- multiple products\n- removing products\n- reviewing selections\n- clear prices\n- clean WhatsApp message generation\n- persistence while navigating\n\nHandle edge cases gracefully.\n\n==================================================\n8. MICRO-DETAILS\n==================================================\n\nThis is where the difference between an average website and an exceptional website should appear.\n\nReview:\n- 1px alignment errors\n- inconsistent spacing\n- typography scale\n- line heights\n- icon sizing\n- button height\n- border treatment\n- corner radii\n- image cropping\n- hover behavior\n- focus states\n- sticky positioning\n- mobile spacing\n- section transitions\n\nMake everything feel systematized.\n\nDo not randomly style individual sections.\n\n==================================================\n9. RESPONSIVE ENGINEERING\n==================================================\n\nThe mobile experience is critical.\n\nDo not simply shrink the desktop layout.\n\nDesign deliberately for:\n\n320px\n360px\n375px\n390px\n430px\n768px\n1024px\n1280px\n1440px+\n\nCheck:\n- overflow\n- wrapping\n- typography\n- navigation\n- filters\n- product grids\n- inquiry drawer\n- sticky controls\n- buttons\n- image proportions\n\nThere must be no awkward breakpoint behavior.\n\n==================================================\n10. PERFORMANCE ENGINEERING\n==================================================\n\nTreat performance as a first-class feature.\n\nAudit:\n- image sizes\n- lazy loading\n- responsive image delivery\n- font loading\n- JavaScript execution\n- unnecessary re-renders\n- bundle size\n- unused dependencies\n- layout shifts\n- animation performance\n\nPrioritize excellent mobile performance.\n\nDo not sacrifice performance for decorative effects.\n\n==================================================\n11. ACCESSIBILITY\n==================================================\n\nImplement professional accessibility standards.\n\nAudit:\n- semantic HTML\n- keyboard navigation\n- focus states\n- form labels\n- aria labels where necessary\n- color contrast\n- buttons vs links\n- image alt text\n- drawer/modal accessibility\n- touch target sizes\n\nThe interface should be usable, not merely attractive.\n\n==================================================\n12. SEO + TECHNICAL FOUNDATION\n==================================================\n\nEnsure the site has a strong technical SEO foundation.\n\nAudit:\n- title tags\n- meta descriptions\n- canonical URLs\n- Open Graph\n- sitemap\n- robots.txt\n- semantic headings\n- structured data\n- product metadata\n- local business information\n\nDo not keyword stuff.\n\n==================================================\n13. TRUST WITHOUT FAKE CLAIMS\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use the Google rating/review section.\n\nDo NOT fabricate:\n- reviews\n- testimonials\n- statistics\n- awards\n- customer counts\n- years in business\n- certifications\n- sales numbers\n\nOnly use genuine business information.\n\nFor customer testimonials, use actual reviews supplied by the business.\n\n==================================================\n14. CONTENT HIERARCHY\n==================================================\n\nThe copy should be concise and confident.\n\nDo not write huge paragraphs.\n\nGood fashion websites communicate with restraint.\n\nPrefer:\n\nElegant headline\nShort supporting sentence\nClear CTA\n\nrather than:\n\nLarge blocks of generic marketing text.\n\n==================================================\n15. VISUAL CONSISTENCY\n==================================================\n\nCreate a coherent design system.\n\nStandardize:\n- button variants\n- typography sizes\n- spacing scale\n- card treatment\n- shadows\n- borders\n- radii\n- icon sizing\n- section headings\n- labels\n- badges\n\nDo not invent a new visual rule every time you create a section.\n\n==================================================\n16. ERROR / EMPTY / LOADING STATES\n==================================================\n\nProfessional websites account for failure.\n\nCreate polished states for:\n- no products found\n- sold out products\n- failed inquiry\n- empty inquiry basket\n- loading catalog\n- missing image\n- invalid form submission\n- unavailable product\n\nThese states should feel designed rather than like browser defaults.\n\n==================================================\n17. REMOVE ANYTHING THAT LOOKS AI-GENERATED\n==================================================\n\nDo a final pass specifically looking for common AI-generated website patterns:\n\n- excessive rounded cards\n- too many gradients\n- generic glassmorphism\n- repetitive sections\n- unnecessary icons\n- giant generic headings\n- meaningless statistics\n- fake testimonials\n- excessive badges\n- random decorative shapes\n- excessive shadows\n- overly dramatic animations\n- repetitive CTAs\n- generic marketing language\n\nRemove them.\n\n==================================================\n18. FINAL 10% POLISH\n==================================================\n\nAfter the main implementation is finished, do another pass.\n\nThen another pass.\n\nLook for details most developers overlook.\n\nAsk:\n\nDoes the header feel right?\n\nDoes the hero composition feel balanced?\n\nDoes the first scroll feel intentional?\n\nDo product images align perfectly?\n\nAre prices visually consistent?\n\nDo buttons feel premium?\n\nDoes the mobile sticky bar feel integrated?\n\nDoes the inquiry drawer feel polished?\n\nDo hover states feel natural?\n\nDo page transitions feel smooth?\n\nAre there awkward empty spaces?\n\nAre any sections repetitive?\n\nDoes the footer feel finished?\n\nWould an experienced designer notice anything amateur?\n\nFix everything you find.\n\n==================================================\n19. DO NOT OVERENGINEER\n==================================================\n\nHigh-quality engineering does NOT mean adding complexity.\n\nDo not introduce:\n- unnecessary databases\n- unnecessary authentication\n- unnecessary state management\n- unnecessary APIs\n- unnecessary dependencies\n- unnecessary animations\n- unnecessary abstractions\n\nUse the simplest architecture that provides a robust production-quality result.\n\n==================================================\n20. FINAL STANDARD\n==================================================\n\nThe final website should feel like:\n\nA professional digital studio spent significant time designing it.\n\nA principal engineer reviewed the architecture.\n\nA senior product designer reviewed the UX.\n\nA creative director reviewed the visual language.\n\nA performance engineer reviewed the frontend.\n\nA QA engineer tested the interactions and responsive behavior.\n\nThe website should communicate craftsmanship.\n\nNOT:\n\n\342\200\234I generated a website.\342\200\235\n\nBUT:\n\n\342\200\234This website was deliberately engineered, designed, tested, and refined.\342\200\235\n\nDo a complete final audit of the existing project and IMPLEMENT the improvements.\n\nDo not merely provide recommendations.\n\nActually make the changes in the codebase."
    items {
      text: "FINAL SENIOR ENGINEERING + DESIGN DIRECTIVE\n\nTreat the current Pearls Elegance Thrift Abaya Wears website as a production project being reviewed by a team of top-tier engineers, product designers, UX specialists, performance engineers, and creative directors.\n\nDo NOT make superficial cosmetic changes.\n\nI want the final website to give the impression:\n\n\342\200\234An exceptional engineering and design team worked obsessively on every detail.\342\200\235\n\nThe quality should be visible in both the DESIGN and the ENGINEERING.\n\n==================================================\n1. THINK LIKE A PRINCIPAL ENGINEER\n==================================================\n\nBefore changing anything, inspect the existing codebase carefully.\n\nUnderstand:\n- application structure\n- components\n- state management\n- product data\n- routing\n- responsive behavior\n- WhatsApp integration\n- inquiry basket\n- SEO implementation\n- image handling\n- dependencies\n- loading behavior\n- error states\n\nDo not blindly rewrite working code.\n\nKeep strong existing architecture.\n\nRefactor only where doing so creates a real improvement.\n\nEliminate:\n- duplicated logic\n- unnecessary components\n- dead code\n- inconsistent patterns\n- fragile state\n- unnecessary dependencies\n- console errors\n- warnings\n- broken imports\n- hardcoded behavior that should be reusable\n\nCreate clean, predictable, maintainable code.\n\n==================================================\n2. THINK LIKE A STAFF / PRINCIPAL FRONTEND ENGINEER\n==================================================\n\nEvery interaction should feel deliberate.\n\nAudit:\n- navigation\n- buttons\n- product cards\n- filters\n- search\n- inquiry drawer\n- WhatsApp flow\n- modals\n- forms\n- sticky elements\n- mobile navigation\n- page transitions\n- hover states\n- loading states\n- empty states\n- error states\n\nNothing should feel accidental.\n\nEvery interaction should have:\n- clear feedback\n- appropriate timing\n- correct state handling\n- accessibility\n- mobile support\n\nDo not add animation just for the sake of animation.\n\n==================================================\n3. THINK LIKE A WORLD-CLASS PRODUCT DESIGNER\n==================================================\n\nThe layout must have intentional hierarchy.\n\nAsk of every section:\n\nWhy is this here?\n\nWhat should the user notice first?\n\nWhat action should the user take?\n\nDoes this section deserve this much visual space?\n\nDoes it contribute to the shopping experience?\n\nRemove anything that does not serve a purpose.\n\nUse:\n- strong visual hierarchy\n- exceptional spacing\n- consistent alignment\n- disciplined typography\n- controlled contrast\n- visual rhythm\n- intentional whitespace\n\nThe site should feel designed, not assembled.\n\n==================================================\n4. PREMIUM FASHION ART DIRECTION\n==================================================\n\nThe visual language must remain:\n\nPremium\nEditorial\nMinimal\nElegant\nModern\nWarm\nSophisticated\n\nClothing MUST remain on mannequins / dress forms.\n\nNO human models.\n\nProduct photography should feel like a professional fashion studio.\n\nUse consistent:\n- lighting\n- composition\n- image ratios\n- background treatment\n- scale\n- cropping\n\nDo not use random images merely to fill empty areas.\n\nEvery image should have a reason to exist.\n\n==================================================\n5. MAKE THE HOMEPAGE FEEL EXPENSIVE\n==================================================\n\nThe homepage should not feel like:\n\nHero\n\342\206\222 cards\n\342\206\222 cards\n\342\206\222 cards\n\342\206\222 footer\n\nInstead, create visual storytelling.\n\nRecommended rhythm:\n\nEDITORIAL HERO\n\n\342\206\223\nNEW ARRIVALS\n\n\342\206\223\nCURATED COLLECTIONS\n\n\342\206\223\nEDITORIAL BRAND STATEMENT\n\n\342\206\223\nSELECTED PRODUCTS\n\n\342\206\223\nCUSTOMER TESTIMONIALS\n\n\342\206\223\nWHOLESALE / RESELLER\n\n\342\206\223\nSTORE LOCATION\n\n\342\206\223\nFINAL CALL TO ACTION\n\nEach section should have a distinct visual purpose.\n\nAvoid repeating the same card design endlessly.\n\n==================================================\n6. PRODUCT EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nThis is still an e-commerce/catalog website.\n\nProduct discovery must be excellent.\n\nMake it extremely easy to:\n\nDiscover\n\342\206\222 Browse\n\342\206\222 Filter\n\342\206\222 Open product\n\342\206\222 Check price\n\342\206\222 Add to inquiry\n\342\206\222 Contact seller\n\nProduct pages should feel intentional and premium.\n\nUse large photography.\n\nDo not overwhelm shoppers with UI.\n\nThe product is the hero.\n\n==================================================\n7. WHATSAPP EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nThe WhatsApp integration should feel like a first-class commerce feature.\n\nDo not make it look like an external link slapped onto the site.\n\nThe flow should feel seamless:\n\nORDER ON WHATSAPP\n\n\342\206\223\n\nPre-filled message\n\n\342\206\223\n\nCustomer confirms availability with Pearls Elegance\n\nThe inquiry basket should feel like a sophisticated lightweight cart.\n\nIt should support:\n- multiple products\n- removing products\n- reviewing selections\n- clear prices\n- clean WhatsApp message generation\n- persistence while navigating\n\nHandle edge cases gracefully.\n\n==================================================\n8. MICRO-DETAILS\n==================================================\n\nThis is where the difference between an average website and an exceptional website should appear.\n\nReview:\n- 1px alignment errors\n- inconsistent spacing\n- typography scale\n- line heights\n- icon sizing\n- button height\n- border treatment\n- corner radii\n- image cropping\n- hover behavior\n- focus states\n- sticky positioning\n- mobile spacing\n- section transitions\n\nMake everything feel systematized.\n\nDo not randomly style individual sections.\n\n==================================================\n9. RESPONSIVE ENGINEERING\n==================================================\n\nThe mobile experience is critical.\n\nDo not simply shrink the desktop layout.\n\nDesign deliberately for:\n\n320px\n360px\n375px\n390px\n430px\n768px\n1024px\n1280px\n1440px+\n\nCheck:\n- overflow\n- wrapping\n- typography\n- navigation\n- filters\n- product grids\n- inquiry drawer\n- sticky controls\n- buttons\n- image proportions\n\nThere must be no awkward breakpoint behavior.\n\n==================================================\n10. PERFORMANCE ENGINEERING\n==================================================\n\nTreat performance as a first-class feature.\n\nAudit:\n- image sizes\n- lazy loading\n- responsive image delivery\n- font loading\n- JavaScript execution\n- unnecessary re-renders\n- bundle size\n- unused dependencies\n- layout shifts\n- animation performance\n\nPrioritize excellent mobile performance.\n\nDo not sacrifice performance for decorative effects.\n\n==================================================\n11. ACCESSIBILITY\n==================================================\n\nImplement professional accessibility standards.\n\nAudit:\n- semantic HTML\n- keyboard navigation\n- focus states\n- form labels\n- aria labels where necessary\n- color contrast\n- buttons vs links\n- image alt text\n- drawer/modal accessibility\n- touch target sizes\n\nThe interface should be usable, not merely attractive.\n\n==================================================\n12. SEO + TECHNICAL FOUNDATION\n==================================================\n\nEnsure the site has a strong technical SEO foundation.\n\nAudit:\n- title tags\n- meta descriptions\n- canonical URLs\n- Open Graph\n- sitemap\n- robots.txt\n- semantic headings\n- structured data\n- product metadata\n- local business information\n\nDo not keyword stuff.\n\n==================================================\n13. TRUST WITHOUT FAKE CLAIMS\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use the Google rating/review section.\n\nDo NOT fabricate:\n- reviews\n- testimonials\n- statistics\n- awards\n- customer counts\n- years in business\n- certifications\n- sales numbers\n\nOnly use genuine business information.\n\nFor customer testimonials, use actual reviews supplied by the business.\n\n==================================================\n14. CONTENT HIERARCHY\n==================================================\n\nThe copy should be concise and confident.\n\nDo not write huge paragraphs.\n\nGood fashion websites communicate with restraint.\n\nPrefer:\n\nElegant headline\nShort supporting sentence\nClear CTA\n\nrather than:\n\nLarge blocks of generic marketing text.\n\n==================================================\n15. VISUAL CONSISTENCY\n==================================================\n\nCreate a coherent design system.\n\nStandardize:\n- button variants\n- typography sizes\n- spacing scale\n- card treatment\n- shadows\n- borders\n- radii\n- icon sizing\n- section headings\n- labels\n- badges\n\nDo not invent a new visual rule every time you create a section.\n\n==================================================\n16. ERROR / EMPTY / LOADING STATES\n==================================================\n\nProfessional websites account for failure.\n\nCreate polished states for:\n- no products found\n- sold out products\n- failed inquiry\n- empty inquiry basket\n- loading catalog\n- missing image\n- invalid form submission\n- unavailable product\n\nThese states should feel designed rather than like browser defaults.\n\n==================================================\n17. REMOVE ANYTHING THAT LOOKS AI-GENERATED\n==================================================\n\nDo a final pass specifically looking for common AI-generated website patterns:\n\n- excessive rounded cards\n- too many gradients\n- generic glassmorphism\n- repetitive sections\n- unnecessary icons\n- giant generic headings\n- meaningless statistics\n- fake testimonials\n- excessive badges\n- random decorative shapes\n- excessive shadows\n- overly dramatic animations\n- repetitive CTAs\n- generic marketing language\n\nRemove them.\n\n==================================================\n18. FINAL 10% POLISH\n==================================================\n\nAfter the main implementation is finished, do another pass.\n\nThen another pass.\n\nLook for details most developers overlook.\n\nAsk:\n\nDoes the header feel right?\n\nDoes the hero composition feel balanced?\n\nDoes the first scroll feel intentional?\n\nDo product images align perfectly?\n\nAre prices visually consistent?\n\nDo buttons feel premium?\n\nDoes the mobile sticky bar feel integrated?\n\nDoes the inquiry drawer feel polished?\n\nDo hover states feel natural?\n\nDo page transitions feel smooth?\n\nAre there awkward empty spaces?\n\nAre any sections repetitive?\n\nDoes the footer feel finished?\n\nWould an experienced designer notice anything amateur?\n\nFix everything you find.\n\n==================================================\n19. DO NOT OVERENGINEER\n==================================================\n\nHigh-quality engineering does NOT mean adding complexity.\n\nDo not introduce:\n- unnecessary databases\n- unnecessary authentication\n- unnecessary state management\n- unnecessary APIs\n- unnecessary dependencies\n- unnecessary animations\n- unnecessary abstractions\n\nUse the simplest architecture that provides a robust production-quality result.\n\n==================================================\n20. FINAL STANDARD\n==================================================\n\nThe final website should feel like:\n\nA professional digital studio spent significant time designing it.\n\nA principal engineer reviewed the architecture.\n\nA senior product designer reviewed the UX.\n\nA creative director reviewed the visual language.\n\nA performance engineer reviewed the frontend.\n\nA QA engineer tested the interactions and responsive behavior.\n\nThe website should communicate craftsmanship.\n\nNOT:\n\n\342\200\234I generated a website.\342\200\235\n\nBUT:\n\n\342\200\234This website was deliberately engineered, designed, tested, and refined.\342\200\235\n\nDo a complete final audit of the existing project and IMPLEMENT the improvements.\n\nDo not merely provide recommendations.\n\nActually make the changes in the codebase."
    }
  }
}
